PLACES=[
N('paris-tabernacle-id','Bear Lake Stake Tabernacle (Paris Tabernacle)','Paris','id','worship','standing',42.22583,-111.40028,'exact','open','drive','','1884–1889',
  'A Romanesque meetinghouse of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, designed by Joseph Don Carlos Young, a son of Brigham Young, and built from 1884 to 1889 by the settlers of the Bear Lake Valley (Wikipedia; the church\'s page). They quarried red sandstone at Indian Creek and hauled it by team; in winter they carted it across the frozen lake to shorten the trip (church\'s page). A fire damaged the interior before the planned 1888 dedication, and it was dedicated in 1889 (Wikipedia). Its size is given differently: about 2,000 seats in the Wikipedia article, 3,000 on the church\'s page.',
  'A large red sandstone church on Paris\'s main street. Inside, the church\'s page describes a sloping floor, a second-floor gallery and a semicircular choir loft; Wikipedia\'s pictures show a vestry, rare in this church, and doors with wood grain painted on. It was refurbished in 2004 and 2005 and is still used for meetings (Wikipedia). Guided tours of about 30 minutes are given (church\'s page).',
  'Red sandstone dragged across a frozen lake to build a pioneer tabernacle.',
  'Owned by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, whose page says the tabernacle is open for public tours and to contact it for current hours; no days or times are on that page. 109 South Main Street; street parking on Main Street (US 89).',
  [W('Bear Lake Stake Tabernacle'),['The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: Paris Tabernacle','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/learn/historic-sites/idaho/paris-tabernacle'],NR('72000436','Bear Lake Stake Tabernacle')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='?'),
N('hatch-house-franklin-id','Lorenzo Hill Hatch House, Franklin','Franklin','id','landmark','standing',42.01806,-111.80222,'site','open','drive','','1874–',
  'A two-storey stone house in the Greek Revival style, built in 1874 for Lorenzo Hill Hatch, the town\'s bishop, and the largest house in Franklin when it was new (Wikipedia; the Idaho State Historical Society). In 1910 the governor proclaimed Idaho Day in honour of Franklin, after its Idaho Pioneer Association gathered settlers\' sworn statements that it was Idaho\'s oldest town (Historical Society). The stone for most of Franklin\'s stone houses came from a small hill northeast of town, cut by immigrant English masons (Historical Society).',
  'A gable-end stone house with a three-bay front, cut-stone quoins at the corners and spare wooden detailing (Wikipedia; Historical Society). The Historical Society has put exhibits in it and in the nearby Doney House, a part-surviving stone house of about 1870 that it saved from demolition in 2002 and moved. Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Idaho State Historical Society and run with the Franklin Pioneer Association. Free; donations welcome. Its page says the buildings open Memorial Day to Labor Day or on request by telephone. Street parking on Main Street (US 91).',
  [W('Franklin Historic Properties'),ISHS_F,NR('73000684','L.H. Hatch House')],
  brick='no',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day: Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 2:30 p.m.; other times on request'),
N('franklin-relic-hall-coop','Relic Hall and Co-operative Mercantile building, Franklin','Franklin','id','civic','standing',42.01739,-111.80088,'site','open','drive','','1869–1937',
  'Two neighbours on Franklin\'s Main Street. The Franklin Co-operative Mercantile Institution was built in 1869 as one of the co-operative general stores the Latter-day Saint leadership set up across its settlements (Wikipedia). In 1923 the Idaho Pioneer Association bought it for a museum, and when the collection outgrew it the state built the log Relic Hall, designed by Chris Gunderson in 1935 and finished in 1937 (Historical Society; Wikipedia, which says the Civilian Conservation Corps built it).',
  'The Relic Hall is a Depression-era rustic log hall on a stone foundation, with a shingle roof and exposed rafters, holding the town\'s pioneer collections (Wikipedia; Historical Society). The old Co-op building, between it and the Hatch House, is not open: the Historical Society invites visitors to look at its outside.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Idaho State Historical Society and run with the Franklin Pioneer Association. Free; donations welcome. The Relic Hall opens with the other Franklin buildings, Memorial Day to Labor Day or on request (Historical Society page). Street parking on Main Street (US 91).',
  [W('Franklin Historic Properties'),ISHS_F,NR('91001717','Franklin Co-operative Mercantile Institution'),NR('00001627','Relic Hall')],
  brick='?',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day: Tuesday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 2:30 p.m.; other times on request'),
N('oneida-stake-academy-preston','Oneida Stake Academy, Preston','Preston','id','civic','standing',42.09599,-111.87451,'site','view','drive','','1890–1895',
  'A church secondary school of the Latter-day Saints\' Oneida Stake, which ran from 1888 to 1922; Ezra Taft Benson and Harold B. Lee studied there (Wikipedia). Wikipedia says the building went up in 1895; the foundation restoring it says it was built between 1890 and 1894 and dedicated in 1895. Public schools used it until 1990, and in 2003 the hand-cut stone building was moved two and a half blocks to Benson Park to save it from demolition (Wikipedia; foundation\'s page).',
  'A large hand-cut stone school building, under restoration by the Oneida Stake Academy Foundation, which means to open it as a museum and cultural centre (foundation\'s page). Not open inside when this was written; look from the park. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'In Benson Park, Preston. The foundation\'s page gives no opening times and speaks of future tours. Street parking round the park.',
  [W('Oneida Stake Academy'),['Oneida Stake Academy Foundation','https://oneidastakeacademy.org/'],NR('75000630','Oneida Stake Academy')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=[42.09709,-111.87515],thn='East Oneida Street, by Benson Park'),
N('chesterfield-ghost-town-id','Chesterfield historic townsite','Chesterfield','id','town','standing',42.86519,-111.90072,'area','open','drive','','1881–',
  'A Latter-day Saint farm town in Gem Valley, on a route of the Oregon Trail, settled from 1879 to 1882 after Chester Call and Christian Nelson ran horses there (Wikipedia; Ensign Peak Foundation). It had more than 400 people at its peak; when the railroad went through Bancroft instead, and farming proved hard, it emptied, and it was deserted by the end of the 1930s (Wikipedia; Ensign Peak Foundation). The nonprofit Chesterfield Foundation, formed in the late 1970s, has preserved or restored the meetinghouse, amusement hall, tithing office and granary (Ensign Peak Foundation).',
  'A Register historic district of 41 buildings and eight sites spread over 2,160 acres, some in Greek Revival and Queen Anne styles, with a cemetery (Wikipedia). The restored meetinghouse is a Daughters of Utah Pioneers museum (Ensign Peak Foundation). Guided tours and a museum are offered (Wikipedia).',
  'A whole pioneer town left standing in an empty valley.',
  'Looked after by the Chesterfield Foundation, a nonprofit. Admission free, according to the Ensign Peak Foundation\'s page, which gives the telephone numbers; the foundation\'s own site could not be opened when this was written, so hours not checked. Several buildings may be private: keep to the roads unless on a tour. Gravel roads; about 10 miles north of Bancroft.',
  [W('Chesterfield, Idaho'),['Ensign Peak Foundation: Chesterfield Historic Town Site','https://ensignpeakfoundation.org/chesterfield-historic-town-site'],NR('80001297','Chesterfield Historic District')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('standrod-house-pocatello','Standrod House (Stanrod Castle), Pocatello','Pocatello','id','landmark','standing',42.865325,-112.457392,'exact','view','drive','','1890s–1902',
  'A turreted sandstone mansion built for Drew W. Standrod, judge and banker, and his wife Emma (SAH Archipedia). Wikipedia and the Register form date it 1897; SAH Archipedia says the San Francisco architect Marcus Grundfor\'s design took about nine years to build and was finished in 1902. The City of Pocatello bought and restored it in 1974 as a museum and events centre; it went back to private use in 1995 and is a house again (SAH Archipedia).',
  'Rusticated grey and pinkish sandstone quarried at McCammon, two round corner towers, pinnacle turrets, a monogrammed "S" above the stair bay and a porch of turned balusters and brackets (Register form; SAH Archipedia). Look from the street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A private home: view from North Garfield Avenue only. 648 North Garfield Avenue, in the Old Town residential district two blocks west of Main Street. Street parking.',
  [W('Standrod House'),['SAH Archipedia: Standrod Mansion','https://sah-archipedia.org/buildings/ID-01-005-0045'],NR('73000680','Standrod House')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('rexburg-tabernacle-id','Rexburg Stake Tabernacle (Tabernacle Civic Center)','Rexburg','id','worship','standing',43.827406,-111.784464,'exact','view','drive','','1911–1912',
  'A Latter-day Saint tabernacle of rusticated local stone, built in 1911 and dedicated on January 7, 1912 (Register form; Idaho Heritage Trust). The Teton Dam flood of 1976 wrecked most of Rexburg\'s pioneer buildings; the townspeople raised money to buy the tabernacle and repair it (Idaho Heritage Trust). It is the city\'s civic centre.',
  'An Italianate church front: two square towers with tall round-arched windows, a three-arched entrance and a curved, Spanish-looking gable over the porch (Register form). The stone came in two shades, from the Rexburg Bench and the Lyman area; the 1,400-seat auditorium was designed by Otto Erlandson of Payson, Utah (Idaho Heritage Trust). The Heritage Trust page says the basement houses the Teton Dam flood museum; whether that is still so, and the interior, not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Rexburg; the city\'s tabernacle page could not be read when this was written, so opening times not checked. Look from North Center Street. A small lot on North Center Street beside it (OpenStreetMap).',
  [NR('74000745','Rexburg Stake Tabernacle'),['Idaho Heritage Trust: Rexburg Tabernacle Civic Center','https://www.idahoheritagetrust.org/?p=4190']],
  brick='no',th=[43.82768,-111.78462],thn='Lot on North Center Street'),
N('birch-creek-charcoal-kilns','Birch Creek Charcoal Kilns','Leadore','id','industry','ruins',44.31152,-113.17889,'exact','public','walk','','1886–1888',
  'Sixteen beehive kilns built in 1886 by Warren King of Butte, Montana, of brick made from local clay, to make charcoal for the smelter at Nicholia, which treated ore from the Viola mine (Wikipedia). Each load of 30 to 40 cords of Douglas fir gave about 1,500 to 2,000 bushels of charcoal in a two-day burn, and 150 to 200 people worked there at the peak; the Viola ore ran out in 1888 and the kilns worked less than three years (Wikipedia).',
  'Four kilns survive, each about 20 feet tall and 20 feet across, at a Forest Service interpretive site (Wikipedia). Nicholia, the smelter town, has only a few ruins; nothing is left of Woodland, the kiln camp (Wikipedia).',
  'Brick beehives in the sagebrush, the last of sixteen that fed a smelter for three years.',
  'Caribou-Targhee National Forest, which lists the kilns among the forest\'s sights and runs them as a public interpretive site (Wikipedia; Forest Service). Reached by a dirt road west of Idaho Highway 28 in the Birch Creek valley; the Forest Service\'s own page for the site was not found, so road and season not checked.',
  [W('Birch Creek Charcoal Kilns'),['Caribou-Targhee National Forest: explore the forest','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r04/caribou-targhee/recreation/explore-forest'],NR('72001577','Charcoal Kilns')],
  brick='?'),
N('custer-ghost-town-id','Custer ghost town','Custer','id','town','standing',44.3875,-114.69583,'site','public','walk','','1877–1910',
  'A gold camp on the Yankee Fork of the Salmon River, begun in 1877 (Wikipedia). Kept going by the Lucky Boy and Black mines, it peaked at about 600 people in 1896; neighbouring Bonanza was burned out in 1889 and 1897, and by 1910 Custer was a ghost town (Forest Service). In the 1930s jobless men and their families moved into the empty town to look for gold (Forest Service).',
  'A Register historic district of 29 acres, of which seven buildings keep enough of their old fabric to count (Wikipedia). The schoolhouse is the Custer Museum, begun in 1960, and the Empire Saloon sells gifts and refreshments in summer; there are houses and shops of the 1880s and 1930s along a walking tour (Forest Service).',
  '',
  'In Land of the Yankee Fork State Park and the Salmon-Challis National Forest (Wikipedia; Forest Service). Free guided and self-guided walking tours Memorial Day to Labor Day in business hours, run with the Friends of Custer Museum (Forest Service). On Yankee Fork Road southwest of Challis; lodging about 21 miles away in Stanley.',
  [W('Custer, Idaho'),['Salmon-Challis National Forest: Custer Ghost Town','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r04/salmon-challis/recreation/discover-history/custer-ghost-town'],NR('81000207','Custer Historic District')],
  brick='?',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day, business hours (guided and self-guided tours)'),
N('albion-normal-school','Albion State Normal School campus','Albion','id','inst','standing',42.41333,-113.58389,'area','view','drive','','1893–1951',
  'One of Idaho\'s two state teacher-training colleges, set up in 1893; Albion\'s people gave land and labour for the campus (Wikipedia). The legislature voted to move it to Burley in 1921, it became the Southern Idaho College of Education in 1947, and the state closed it in 1951. Magic Valley Christian College used the campus from 1957 to 1969; it then went to the town, stood empty and decaying, and was sold at auction in 2007 to a family who began to rehabilitate part of it as a retreat and conference centre (Wikipedia).',
  'A campus district on the National Register since 1980, including Swanger Hall of 1893, Comish Hall, Miller Hall and the gymnasium (Wikipedia). It is now the privately run Albion Campus Retreat, and seasonal "Haunted Mansions of Albion" tours are held there (Wikipedia). Which buildings stand empty, and their insides, not checked.',
  'A state college town that lost its college.',
  'Private: view from Albion\'s streets unless booked through the retreat. Albion is on Idaho Highway 77 south of Burley.',
  [W('Albion State Normal School'),NR('80001298','Albion Normal School Campus')],
  brick='?'),
N('rock-creek-station-stricker','Rock Creek Station and Stricker Homesite','Hansen','id','hotel','standing',42.45953,-114.32196,'site','open','drive','','1864–1897',
  'Where the Oregon Trail and the Kelton freight road met, Ben Holladay chose a home station for his Overland Stage Line in 1864; it became the largest stage station between Fort Hall and Fort Boise (Wikipedia). James Bascom built the Rock Creek store in 1865, and Herman Stricker bought it in 1876 and kept it until it closed in 1897 (Wikipedia).',
  'The Rock Creek store building, the Stricker house, a rebuilt summer house, a pioneer cemetery, outbuildings and an interpretive centre (Idaho State Historical Society). Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Idaho State Historical Society and run with the Friends of Stricker. Free; donations welcome. Self-guided tours daily; the buildings open on Sunday afternoons; group tours by arrangement (Historical Society page). 3715 East 3200 North, south of Hansen.',
  [W('Rock Creek Station and Stricker Homesite'),['Idaho State Historical Society: Rock Creek Station and Stricker Homesite','https://history.idaho.gov/stricker/']],
  brick='?',hours='Grounds daily (self-guided); buildings Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.'),
N('minidoka-nhs-id','Minidoka National Historic Site','Hunt','id','inst','ruins',42.679092,-114.24373,'site','public','walk','','1942–1945',
  'One of the ten camps where Japanese Americans removed from the West Coast were held during the Second World War, mostly people from Oregon, Washington and Alaska; more than 13,000 passed through and 9,397 were held there at the peak (Wikipedia). Each block had twelve barracks, a laundry, bathrooms and a mess hall (Wikipedia). It is a National Historic Site run by the National Park Service.',
  'The remains of the entry guard station and waiting room, a rock garden, and a recreated Honor Roll of the camp\'s servicemen (Wikipedia). The visitor center is in warehouse number 5, the camp\'s motor repair shop (National Park Service). A 1.6-mile trail with outdoor signs runs past the historic structures.',
  '',
  'National Park Service. The walking trail is open sunrise to sunset every day; pets are not allowed on it (National Park Service). The visitor center opens in the warmer months only; check the park\'s page. Lot on Hunt Road by the visitor center. 1428 Hunt Road, northeast of Twin Falls.',
  [W('Minidoka National Historic Site'),['National Park Service: Minidoka hours','https://www.nps.gov/miin/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  brick='?',hours='Trail sunrise to sunset daily; visitor center seasonal',th=[42.67866,-114.24418],thn='Hunt Road lot, Minidoka visitor center'),
N('st-josephs-mission-slickpoo','St. Joseph\'s Mission (Slickpoo)','Culdesac','id','worship','standing',46.31465,-116.71022,'site','view','drive','','1874–1958',
  'A wood-frame mission church on Mission Creek; the sanctuary was built in 1874 and the nave lengthened in 1904 (Wikipedia). The mission closed in 1958 and most of its buildings are gone; the chapel has been kept, no longer in use (National Park Service). It is an affiliated site of Nez Perce National Historical Park.',
  'The white frame chapel, with an interpretive sign in front of it (National Park Service). The inside is not open to the public. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Within the Nez Perce Reservation, on private property; the National Park Service says the chapel\'s interior is not open and visitors may read the sign in front. About 4 miles up Mission Creek Road off US 95, some 6.5 miles southwest of Culdesac (National Park Service). Stay on the road.',
  [W('St. Joseph\'s Mission (Culdesac, Idaho)'),['National Park Service: Visit Saint Joseph\'s Mission','https://www.nps.gov/nepe/planyourvisit/visit-saint-joseph-mission.htm']],
  brick='no'),
N('st-gertrude-monastery-cottonwood','Monastery of St. Gertrude (convent and chapel)','Cottonwood','id','worship','standing',46.03372,-116.39130,'exact','open','drive','','1919–1924',
  'The Benedictine sisters of St. Gertrude\'s began their present convent in 1919, and the chapel was dedicated on July 25, 1924 (Register form). They ran a Catholic high school there from 1927 to 1970 (Register form). The Historical Museum at St. Gertrude, begun by Sister Alfreda Elsensohn as an attic museum, keeps the sisters\' story and that of the Camas Prairie and the Salmon River country (museum\'s page).',
  'A three-storey Romanesque group of blue porphyry stone quarried nearby: a basilica-plan chapel with two 97-foot corner towers capped by Bavarian-style domes, and a convent wing to the south, with a later brick addition (Register form); a 2019 photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows red brick round the chapel\'s window arches. The form singles out the altar, made in Sigmaringen, Germany in 1928, and blue and gold painting on its canopy. Whether the chapel is open to visitors not checked; the museum is.',
  'Bavarian towers of local stone on the Camas Prairie.',
  'The monastery belongs to the Benedictine sisters, who also run an inn and retreat centre there. The museum: adults $6, students $3, under 6 free; closed Sundays and some holidays (museum\'s page). 465 Keuterville Road, west of Cottonwood.',
  [NR('79000790','St. Gertrude\'s Convent and Chapel'),['Historical Museum at St. Gertrude','https://historicalmuseumatstgertrude.org/']],
  brick='some',hours='Museum Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., all year; closed Sundays and some holidays'),
N('mcconnell-mansion-moscow','McConnell Mansion, Moscow','Moscow','id','landmark','standing',46.73321,-116.99686,'exact','open','walk','','1886–',
  'Built in 1886 for William J. McConnell, merchant and later governor of Idaho, whose family lived there until 1897 (Wikipedia; Latah County Historical Society). Later owners took in boarders; the last, Dr. Frederic Church of the University of Idaho, left it to Latah County in 1966, and it opened as a museum in 1970 (Wikipedia).',
  'A Stick and Eastlake style house in the Fort Russell neighbourhood (Wikipedia). The main floor has rooms set out as they were between 1900 and the 1930s, and the restored master bedroom and exhibits are upstairs (Historical Society).',
  '',
  'Run by the Latah County Historical Society for the county; staffed by volunteers, so Saturday opening should be checked by telephone or email (Historical Society page). 110 South Adams Street; street parking.',
  [W('W. J. McConnell House'),['Latah County Historical Society: McConnell Mansion','https://www.latahcountyhistoricalsociety.org/hours--address'],NR('74000743','W. J. McConnell House')],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday to Friday 1 to 4 p.m., and some Saturdays'),
N('intermountain-institute-weiser','Intermountain Institute (Hooker Hall), Weiser','Weiser','id','civic','standing',44.264361,-116.980784,'site','open','drive','','1899–1967',
  'A school founded in 1899 by the Rev. Edward Paddock, Jane Slocum and Thomas Maryatt, where young people worked five hours a day for their schooling; Theodore Roosevelt and George Eastman were among its donors (Idaho Heritage Trust). It was ruined by the Depression, its assets went to the College of Idaho and Whitman College, and the Weiser school district used the buildings until 1967 (Idaho Heritage Trust).',
  'Hooker Memorial Hall of 1924, five storeys of concrete scored and cast to look like stone, with a Spanish mission gable and a four-sided clock tower; the campus also has a 1929 gymnasium by Tourtellotte and Hummel that held a swimming pool (Register form). Hooker Hall is the Snake River Heritage Center museum, and the 1880 Harper log cabin was moved onto the grounds in 1996 (Idaho Heritage Trust). Interior not checked.',
  'A school paid for by its students\' labour, and by Roosevelt.',
  'The Snake River Heritage Center occupies Hooker Hall (Idaho Heritage Trust); no page of its own with hours was read, so hours not checked. 2295 Paddock Avenue, on the hill north of downtown Weiser.',
  [NR('79000811','Intermountain Institute'),['Idaho Heritage Trust: Snake River Heritage Center','https://www.idahoheritagetrust.org/?p=3826']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('sandpoint-depot-id','Sandpoint station (Northern Pacific depot)','Sandpoint','id','rail','standing',48.27644,-116.54559,'exact','open','walk','stations','1916–',
  'The Northern Pacific\'s depot of 1916, the oldest of its passenger depots still in use and the only working Amtrak station in Idaho since 1997 (Wikipedia; Register form). The Register form calls it the state\'s only Gothic-style railway station. Amtrak restored it in 2015 for $926,000, with a new roof of green tiles like the first ones and a refurbished waiting room (Wikipedia).',
  'A one-storey brick depot with Tudor Gothic trim: brick gables with pointed-arch windows and stone ball finials, a crenellated bay window on the platform side, and Tudor-arched doors into the waiting room (Register form). Interior fabric not checked.',
  '',
  'A working Amtrak stop on the Empire Builder; the site belongs to BNSF Railway (Wikipedia). Keep off the tracks. The waiting room\'s hours not checked. 450 Railroad Avenue; parking near the depot not checked on the aerial.',
  [W('Sandpoint station'),NR('73000682','Sandpoint Burlington Northern Railway Station')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
]

